import{_ as a,P as n,d as i,e as o,y as s,q as r,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.14 Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.14 Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction","xkChars":0,"xkWords":488,"xkLessons":{"D.14":{"title":"D.14 Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction","lead":"Upholding an original decision after seriously reviewing an appeal and upholding it without reviewing it at all produce the same outward result but differ in quality. Research on institutions and power must trace how reasons, materials, and authority operate, rather than merely count how many changes occur.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 4825–4840; source anchor section-d-14; short quotation, translated: 'The rate of change cannot replace a judgment of quality.' The source passage for this topic is also preserved verbatim in source-slice.md; the explanation is separate from the source.","Formation, maintenance, function, social recognition, legal validity, and normative justification are different questions. How an institution arose is not the same as what maintains it today. An institution's production of a function does not establish that this function was its origin, much less that it is lawful or justified. First list the questions clearly, so that a single label of structural strength or institutional success does not stand in for them all.","Functionalism, interpretive sociology, interactionism, structuration and morphogenesis, studies of power and knowledge, public communication, social systems theory, and actor-network theory retain their respective objects. Relations among outcomes, the meanings of actions, situated order, temporal processes, classification and power, communication, and the participation of things and technologies cannot all be rewritten as one structural quantity. A particular borrowing must attend to the original argument's units and strong commitments, rather than merely collect similar vocabulary.","Public compliance may be motivated by endorsement, habit, dependence on income, threats, or a lack of alternatives. The same action is insufficient to identify which motive applies; actors' reasons, actual options, and constraints need separate examination. Understanding a communication does not establish agreement, and accurately restating a rule does not establish voluntary acceptance. Observable compliance cannot be used to fill in normative endorsement after the fact.","A legal text's provision of reasons and a service's actual delivery are different links. A written decision may correctly invoke rules, while it remains necessary to establish whether resources, personnel, and implementation conditions actually let people receive the service. Things and technologies can also have causal effects: a form or device may change visibility and opportunities to act. This does not give machines or objects the moral responsibilities of people. Causal action positions and responsible parties must be kept separate.","Rules do more than repress; they may also make coordination, predictability, and rights claims possible. Check how rules operate through specific channels: who is classified, how training occurs, what is recorded, how rewards and sanctions work, and who has decision-making and review authority. Criticism cannot stop at saying that power exists, nor can rules' usefulness for coordination cancel investigation of classification and exclusion.","Participation may correct errors, or it may merely increase expression without changing authority. The number of people at a meeting, the number of comments, and expressions of satisfaction do not establish that comments can affect key judgments. An ordinary procedure that seriously understands the materials and reasonably rejects a request may still have value in respect and knowledge; effective participation does not require every demand to be accepted. But hearing voices without actually handling them cannot substitute procedural form for a response.","Three levels of records are needed here: actual causal feedback, the quality of participation in general, and permission to adopt a version under Xikari's own GOV-02. An appeal that changes resource arrangements can support that particular change, while the justification of the distribution requires separate judgment. A demand rejected for reasons can support a claim about the quality of the response in general, but cannot be recorded as an accepted request. Within the framework, applied is also governed by the specific conditions in 16.2; no level automatically certifies another. [Source: theory-gov02]","GOV-02 requires each objection to have a disposition, reasons, the evidence used, and its effects on the text, process, and action ceiling. Effects of acceptance are tied to an actual change_id; reasoned non-acceptance is also retained. applied does not require token acceptance of at least one objection. It requires proportionate handling of all reasons that could change key judgments, protections, or authority. If such a key objection remains unresolved, necessary independent review is missing, or nonwaivable matters have not been satisfied, suspend the affected adoption. Continuing opposition must not automatically be treated as a permanent veto either. Decision criteria should be published in advance, open to appeal, and independently reviewable. [Source: theory-gov02]","Institutions may be repeatedly realized through skilled practice, budgets, tools, informal commitments, and staffing arrangements without amending their constitutive documents every time. Unchanged paperwork alongside reorganized actual responsibilities, or amended paperwork alongside unchanged implementation, both merit study. Institutional reproduction is more than copying a passage of text and need not mean that no change has ever occurred. Examine how actual work continues to be accomplished and where shifts occur.","Upholding a correct decision after review can be distinguished from upholding a decision without real review by asking whether reasons address the materials, whether counterexamples were handled, and whether the relevant authority was present. A low reversal rate does not automatically establish an absence of review, nor does a high rate automatically establish greater fairness: initial error rates, case entry, and standards may differ. The rate of change is a limited description, not a substitute for a judgment of quality. Irrelevant suggestions must not be accepted symbolically merely to meet a change target.","The nine entry points, in order, are social facts and functions in Durkheim, Parsons, and Merton; Weber's interpretive sociology; interaction order in Mead, Blumer, and Goffman; structuration and morphogenesis in Giddens and Archer; Foucault's power and knowledge; the Frankfurt School and Habermas's public communication; Luhmann's systems theory; conflict, integration, and reproduction; and Latour's actor-network theory. The strongest objection is that reasoned refusal may become an excuse for arbitrary refusal. The response is that reasons must face materials, counterexamples, and independent review, rather than being declared sufficient by the decision maker."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Understanding a communication","leftText":"Being able to explain what the other party said.","rightTitle":"Agreement and endorsement","rightText":"Require separate materials about the relevant wishes; compliance may also arise from constraints."},{"leftTitle":"Changes to formal texts","leftText":"Updates to a document's content.","rightTitle":"Reorganization of practice","rightText":"Changes to actual budgets, personnel, tools, and processes may be out of step with paperwork."},{"leftTitle":"A response in general","leftText":"An issue receives a reasoned disposition supported by materials.","rightTitle":"Acceptance of a request","rightText":"Requires confirming that the demand actually was accepted or implemented."},{"leftTitle":"Actual change through feedback","leftText":"A judgment, process, or resource arrangement changes.","rightTitle":"Normative justification","rightText":"Change itself does not establish that the distribution or authority is correct."},{"leftTitle":"GOV-02 applied","leftText":"Satisfying the framework's specific internal adoption contract.","rightTitle":"Token acceptance or permanent veto","rightText":"Neither extreme replaces handling key objections and performing necessary independent review."}],"boundaries":["Do not infer endorsement directly from compliance, or turn the causal effects of things and technologies into human moral responsibility.","Do not collapse all social theories into structural strength, or pretend to have read all the research listed in the directory.","Reasonable refusal in an ordinary procedure and adoption under GOV-02 belong to different levels; unresolved key objections suspend adoption within the affected scope.","Upholding a correct decision for reasons may have value as a response, but the genuineness of those reasons and the quality of review must be testable.","All appeal and institutional examples are teaching constructions; they adjudicate no actual legal dispute and change no actual authority."],"questions":["How can a reasoned refusal in general be distinguished from acceptance of a request?","Why does applied not require token acceptance of at least one objection?","Why can institutions change even when their paperwork does not?","Why can the quality of an appeals process not be assessed solely by its reversal rate?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/figures/section-d-14.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual statement of principles: handling objections in RFC7282","href":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual statement of principles: handling objections in RFC7282","text":"RFC7282 is an Informational document published in June 2014. It explicitly is not a BCP changing IETF procedures. Section 3 explains that objections should be substantively addressed and reasons given for concerns that remain unsatisfied. Continuing dissatisfaction does not necessarily prevent rough consensus, but majority support cannot make up for ignoring an issue. This lesson uses those published principles to distinguish handling a concern from accepting it; the document's hypothetical protocol disputes are not presented as actual meeting events.","sourceLabel":"RFC7282，On Consensus and Humming in the IETF，June2014，Informational","sourceUrl":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html","sourceLocator":"First-page Abstract note and Status; §3, printed pp7–9, especially the paragraphs beginning 'Any finding…' and 'Remember…' on p9 and their continuation on p10","boundary":"The document describes principles; it does not establish that every actual discussion follows them. It does not replace Xikari's specific GOV-02 contract or another organization's statutory authority.","href":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.14","start_line":4916,"end_line":4931,"startLine":4916,"endLine":4931}],"outputId":"section-d-14","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Separate formation, maintenance, function, recognition, legal validity, and normative justification, and locate the units of the theory being borrowed.","Examine action meanings, actual constraints, and service implementation. Do not infer endorsement from compliance or actual delivery from a text.","Trace actual channels through classification, training, recording, rewards and sanctions, and decision-making and review authority.","Record causal feedback, participation in general, and eligibility for adoption under GOV-02 separately. Handle key objections under standards established in advance and open to review.","Check whether paperwork and actual processes agree; assess review reasons and materials rather than merely the rate of change."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Developing the source's teaching example: upholding a decision for reasons","text":"Suppose an applicant appeals and a reviewer checks all key materials, addresses counterexamples, and explains why the original decision still holds. One may record that a response in general and a review actually occurred, but not that the applicant's demand was accepted. If permission for adoption within the framework is also sought, every GOV-02 condition must still be checked; this one appeal cannot stand in for the entire contract.","assumptions":"Constructed from the source, with sufficient materials as an explicit assumption.","boundary":"The sufficiency of actual reasons requires independent examination; the reviewer's unilateral self-certification is not accepted."},{"title":"Developing the source's teaching example: change need not be justified","text":"Suppose another appeal causes an organization to amend a rule and increase a group's resources. This can establish feedback through which a comment enters practice. Whether the adjustment harms other groups or satisfies rights floors and authorization requirements still needs separate judgment. Actual change is not sufficient for a justified distribution.","assumptions":"The institutional resources and groups are fictional.","boundary":"No actual allocation was implemented; change in a teaching example does not establish net improvement."},{"title":"Teaching construction: the same zero reversal rate, two kinds of review","text":"Suppose two hypothetical procedures each handle 10 cases and uphold every decision. In procedure A, the original decisions are all correct and each receives appropriate independent review. Procedure B never reads the materials and simply rubber-stamps the decisions. Both have a reversal rate of 0/10=0, but their procedural and epistemic quality differs. The rate of change cannot by itself identify effective review.","assumptions":"The correctness of the cases and the procedural details are teaching assumptions, not actual judicial statistics.","boundary":"This does not claim that zero reversals are always good or always bad, or that merely providing written reasons is enough."},{"title":"Teaching construction: unchanged constitutive documents, changed implementation","text":"Suppose an institution's constitutive documents remain unchanged while its budget moves from a centralized service counter to mobile services, with staffing schedules and tools changing accordingly. The actual service process can be reorganized. Conversely, amending only the documents without changing resources or personnel does not automatically establish that services have been delivered.","assumptions":"This is not an actual administrative project.","boundary":"Every change and effect needs materials; it cannot be inferred from a document's timestamp."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Opposition remains, so no decision can ever be adopted.","effect":"What matters is whether objections receive proportionate handling under appropriate standards and necessary review, not whether everyone is privately satisfied.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Token acceptance of an irrelevant comment is enough to obtain applied.","effect":"GOV-02 asks whether reasons that could affect key judgments, protections, and authority have been handled; it does not rely on token acceptance.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"All ten decisions were upheld, so the review must have been merely formal.","effect":"The quality of the original decisions and the actual review must be checked. The zero-reversal construction shows why an outcome rate is insufficient.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"How can a reasoned refusal in general be distinguished from acceptance of a request?","answer":"The former concerns the quality of handling; the latter concerns acceptance of a particular demand. One being true does not require the other to be true.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why does applied not require token acceptance of at least one objection?","answer":"The contract requires proportionate handling of key reasons. Genuine acceptance must involve actual change; formal acceptance cannot make up for missing review.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can institutions change even when their paperwork does not?","answer":"Budgets, tools, personnel, skilled practices, and informal commitments can reorganize actual processes.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can the quality of an appeals process not be assessed solely by its reversal rate?","answer":"The same result may come from proper review or no review at all. Materials, reasons, authority, and an appropriate capacity to correct errors must be examined.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/figures/section-d-14.png","alt":"Separate formation, maintenance, function, recognition, legal validity, and normative justification. Compliance ≠ endorsement; text ≠ service delivery; technological causation ≠ human responsibility. Trace actual channels through classification, training, recording, rewards and sanctions, and decision-making and review authority. Keep three levels separate: causal feedback, the quality of participation in general, and GOV-02 permission. Handle key objections proportionately, provide necessary independent review, and check actual changes for acceptance. Paperwork and practice may be out of step; upholding a correct decision can be effective; the rate of change does not replace quality. Drawn by the lesson author from D.14 and 16.2; the appeal and zero-reversal examples are teaching constructions, not actual adjudications of authority.","caption":"Drawn by the lesson author from D.14 and 16.2; the appeal and zero-reversal examples are teaching constructions, not actual adjudications of authority."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static figure; interaction has not been implemented. Any selection, switching, or clicking described below is only a proposal for later design. Switching whether objections were substantively addressed, whether necessary review occurred, and whether actual changes were made would display participation quality and adoption eligibility separately.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The original statement of principles illustrates the distinction between responding and satisfying a demand, while clearly separating its Informational status from permission within the framework."},"openQuestions":["The complete original theoretical texts for 14.01–14.09 were not obtained; disputes within and among those theories were not resolved.","Actual IETF meetings and implementation of appeals were not examined; the case supports only what the original document says."],"taskId":"S-D-14","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research draft, full-text lines 4737–4752; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.14: terminology, contracts, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; these do not certify empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"theory-gov02","title":"Historical Chinese research draft: original book 16.2, GOV-02 consensus procedures and version authority","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese book lines 3290–3310, especially the final paragraph on handling each objection and the conditions for applied","claim_support":["Proportionate handling of key objections, independent review, actual change_id, and suspension of affected adoption"],"limits":["An internal contract of the framework; it does not directly replace other organizations' statutory procedures.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"rfc7282","title":"On Consensus and Humming in the IETF","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7282.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"June2014","locator":"Abstract note and Status; §3, printed pp7–9; 'Any finding…' and 'Remember…' on p9, with continuation on p10","claim_support":["Informational status and not a BCP; objections require substantive handling and reasons when left unsatisfied, rather than substitution by head counts"],"limits":["A statement of principles, not evidence of implementation in an actual case; it cannot replace GOV-02."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"D.14 Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction; Chinese source lines 4825–4840; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-14/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"7e8cbe96b2773288037b4836f5dd1015e4cc44b5d731d644d569d00b3e124233","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-14.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-14.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-14.md"};function l(h,e,u,p,m,f){const t=n("SectionLesson");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=s('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-14" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.14</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-14" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.14 Social Theory, Power, Communication, and Institutional Reproduction {#section-d-14}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §3.3, §7.6, §10–11, §14.5, and §16.2. Research entries cover 14.01–14.09.</p><p>Formation, maintenance, function, social recognition, legal validity, and normative justification are distinct questions. Functionalist accounts of consequences, the meanings of action in interpretive sociology, the order of encounters in interactionism, temporal processes of structuration and morphogenesis, studies of power and knowledge, public communication, social systems theory, and actor–network theory cannot all be reduced to one measure of “structural strength.”</p><p>Visible compliance can arise from endorsement, habit, dependence on income, threats, or lack of alternatives. The same behavior does not suffice to identify a motive. Understanding a message does not amount to agreement. Establishing a legal rationale in writing does not mean services have reached people. The causal effects of objects and technologies do not make them bear human moral responsibility. Rules can constrain action and can also make coordination and claims to rights possible.</p><p>Criticism must trace concrete channels of classification, training, recordkeeping, rewards and penalties, and decision-making authority. Participation can correct mistakes or increase expression without changing permissions. A reasoned refusal of a request through an ordinary procedure can still have value for respect and knowledge. For adoption within the framework, GOV-02 requires each item to be addressed with reasons, independent review where needed, and verification of actual changes. Token acceptance cannot establish <code>applied</code>. The continued presence of objections must not invariably be treated as a permanent veto.</p><p>In a teaching example, an appeal is heard and receives a reasoned response. The original decision stands because it has sufficient evidence. This can establish that a response actually took place, but does not establish that the applicant’s request was granted. Another appeal actually changes rules and resources. The change alone does not establish that the distribution is normatively justified. Record causal feedback, ordinary participation, and authorization to adopt a new version within the framework at three separate levels.</p><p>Institutions can be realized repeatedly through skilled practice, budgets, tools, informal commitments, and personnel arrangements. They need not rewrite their charters each time. Reorganization of actual processes while documents stay the same, and changes to documents while execution stays the same, both have mechanisms that can be studied. Upholding a correct decision after review and leaving a decision in place without genuine review can look the same. They remain distinguishable by reasons, handling of material, and authority. The rate of changes cannot substitute for judging quality.</p><p>Research topics. 14.01 Social facts and functionalism (Durkheim, Parsons, and Merton). 14.02 Interpretive sociology (Weber). 14.03 Symbolic interactionism and everyday order (Mead, Blumer, and Goffman). 14.04 Structuration, morphogenesis, and agency (Giddens and Archer). 14.05 Power, knowledge, discipline, and governmentality (Foucault). 14.06 Critical theory and public communication (the Frankfurt School and Habermas). 14.07 Social systems theory (Luhmann). 14.08 Conflict, integration, and institutional reproduction. 14.09 Actor–network theory and nonhuman positions of action (Latour).</p></div>',1)),r(t,{section:"D.14"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=a(d,[["render",l]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
